/* generated by tools/make-lessons.js from tools/lessons/pca/*.js; do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = Object.assign(window.LESSONS || {}, {"pca/directions":{"idea":"Spin a line through a cloud of points and measure how spread out the points are along it. One angle gives the most spread. That line is the <b>first principal component</b>, and it is also the line the points sit closest to.","why":"Columns that move together store the same information twice. To shrink 13 or 64 columns to a few numbers you must decide which directions to keep. Keep a direction where all customers look alike and you keep nothing. Drop the direction where they spread out and you lose what tells them apart. Maximum variance is the rule that chooses well.","analogy":"People stand in a hall in a diagonal band from the door to the stage. To tell a friend where someone is, one number works best: how far along the diagonal they stand. That is almost everything. The leftover, how far to the side of the diagonal, is small. The diagonal is the principal component, \"along\" is the score, and \"to the side\" is the distance lost.","vocab":[["Variance","The average squared distance from the mean (divided by n − 1). It measures spread, in the unit of the data squared."],["Centring","Subtracting each column's mean so the cloud sits on the origin. PCA always works on centred data."],["Score","Where a point lands when you drop a perpendicular onto a line through the centre: <span class=\"eq\">s = u·x</span>, with u a unit arrow along the line. The length of that perpendicular is the <i>distance lost</i>."],["Covariance matrix C","A table with each column's variance on the diagonal and, off the diagonal, how two columns move together. In 2-D: <span class=\"eq\">C = [[a, b], [b, c]]</span>."],["Eigenvector and eigenvalue","A direction u that C only stretches: <span class=\"eq\">Cu = λu</span>. The stretch λ is the variance of the scores along u."],["Principal component (PC)","A unit-length eigenvector of C. PC1 has the largest λ. PC2 has the next largest and sits at 90° to PC1."],["SVD","Singular value decomposition. For centred data, <span class=\"eq\">X = U S Vᵀ</span>. The rows of Vᵀ are the PCs and <span class=\"eq\">s²/(n−1)</span> are the eigenvalues."],["Share of variance","One eigenvalue divided by the sum of all eigenvalues (the total variance)."]],"steps":[{"h":"Start with two columns that rise together","p":"Each customer has two numbers: monthly income and monthly card spend. They rise together, so the points form a tilted oval. We want <b>one</b> number per customer that keeps as much of the picture as possible. First subtract each column's mean (<b>centring</b>), so the cloud sits on the origin and only its spread is left to describe."},{"h":"Choose a direction and drop every point onto it","p":"A direction is an arrow of length 1 at angle θ: <span class=\"eq\">u = (cos θ, sin θ)</span>. Drop a perpendicular from each point onto the line through the centre along u. Where it lands, measured from the centre, is the point's <b>score</b>, <span class=\"eq\">s = u·x</span>. Each customer now has one number. Every angle gives different scores."},{"h":"Two ways to judge an angle","p":"<b>Kept</b>: the variance of the scores, the spread along the line. <b>Lost</b>: the average squared length of the perpendiculars, how far points sit from the line. For one point, Pythagoras says (along)² + (across)² = (distance from the centre)², and the right side does not depend on the angle. Averaging over points: <b>kept + lost = total variance</b>, at every angle."},{"h":"So the best line wins twice","p":"Because kept + lost is fixed, the angle that makes kept largest makes lost smallest. \"Spreads the points most\" and \"stays closest to the points\" are the same line. In 2-D, with variance a of x, covariance b and variance c of y: <span class=\"eq\">kept(θ) = a cos²θ + 2b sinθ cosθ + c sin²θ</span>. This is a smooth wave that repeats every 180°, because θ and θ + 180° are the same line."},{"h":"The peak of the wave is an eigenvector","p":"Maximising <span class=\"eq\">uᵀCu</span> over unit vectors leads to <span class=\"eq\">Cu = λu</span>: u is an <b>eigenvector</b> of C, and the variance kept is its eigenvalue λ. The wave peaks at the largest eigenvalue λ₁ and bottoms out 90° later at λ₂. In 2-D, <span class=\"eq\">λ = (a+c)/2 ± √(((a−c)/2)² + b²)</span>. The two eigenvalues add up to a + c, the total variance."},{"h":"The SVD gives the same answer","p":"Software rarely builds C. It takes the SVD of the centred data, <span class=\"eq\">X = U S Vᵀ</span>. The rows of Vᵀ are the principal components and the eigenvalues are <span class=\"eq\">λ = s²/(n−1)</span>. This avoids squaring the data, so it is more accurate. The two routes agree to about 1e-15, which the last three readouts show."},{"h":"Scores and share","p":"Each principal component j gives every customer a score <span class=\"eq\">sⱼ = uⱼ·x</span>, and carries the share <span class=\"eq\">λⱼ / Σλ</span> of the total variance. Keeping the first k scores per customer is <b>dimension reduction</b>. The next simulations deal with how the columns are measured (scaling) and how many scores to keep (choosing k)."},{"h":"When PC1 means little","p":"If λ₁ ≈ λ₂ (a round cloud), the wave is almost flat. Every angle keeps nearly the same variance, and PC1 turns whenever the sample changes. PCA also never sees class labels: it finds where points spread, not where classes differ. A few far-away points can drag PC1, because distances enter squared."}],"example":{"title":"five customers, solved by hand","setup":"Monthly income and card spend (₹ thousand) of five customers: <b>(18, 9), (19, 9), (20, 11), (21, 11), (22, 10)</b>. Find PC1.","table":{"head":["Direction","Angle θ","Variance kept","Variance lost (3.5 − kept)","Share kept"],"rows":[["income axis","0°","2.50","1.00","71.4%"],["PC1","26.57°","3.00","0.50","85.7%"],["diagonal","45°","2.75","0.75","78.6%"],["spend axis","90°","1.00","2.50","28.6%"],["PC2","116.57°","0.50","3.00","14.3%"]]},"walk":["<b>Centre.</b> Mean income = 20 and mean spend = 10, so the centred points are (−2, −1), (−1, −1), (0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 0).","<b>Covariance</b> (divide by n − 1 = 4). a = (4+1+0+1+4)/4 = 2.5. c = (1+1+1+1+0)/4 = 1.0. b = (2+1+0+1+0)/4 = 1.0. Total variance = a + c = 3.5.","<b>Try three angles.</b> Along income only (θ = 0°): 2.5. Along spend only (90°): 1.0. Along the diagonal (45°): 2.5·½ + 2·1.0·½ + 1.0·½ = 2.75, better than either axis. So the best line is tilted.","<b>Eigenvalues.</b> λ = (a+c)/2 ± √(((a−c)/2)² + b²) = 1.75 ± √(0.5625 + 1) = 1.75 ± 1.25. So <b>λ₁ = 3.0</b> and <b>λ₂ = 0.5</b>. They add to 3.5, the total.","<b>Eigenvector of λ₁.</b> Solve (a − λ₁)v₁ + b·v₂ = 0: −0.5·v₁ + 1.0·v₂ = 0, so v₂ = 0.5·v₁. The direction is (2, 1). Divide by its length √5 = 2.236 to get length 1: <b>PC1 = (0.894, 0.447)</b>, at angle arctan(0.5) = 26.6°. PC2 is at 90° to it: (−0.447, 0.894).","<b>Check by scoring.</b> s = 0.894·x + 0.447·y gives −2.236, −1.342, 0.447, 1.342, 1.789. Their squares are 5.0, 1.8, 0.2, 1.8, 3.2, which add to 12, and 12/4 = 3.0 = λ₁. On PC2 the scores are 0, −0.447, 0.894, 0.447, −0.894: squares add to 2.0, and 2.0/4 = 0.5 = λ₂.","<b>Share.</b> PC1 keeps 3.0 / 3.5 = 85.7% of the variance; dropping PC2 loses 14.3%."],"result":"PC1 = (0.894, 0.447) at 26.6°, with variance 3.0 out of 3.5. One score per customer, <span class=\"eq\">s = 0.894·(income − 20) + 0.447·(spend − 10)</span>, keeps 85.7% of the spread. This line is steeper than the regression line of spend on income (slope 0.4, angle 21.8°), because PCA measures distance at right angles to the line, while regression measures only the vertical distance."},"read":[["Blue dots and the black cross","The points of the cloud and their mean (the centre). Drag a dot to move it, click an empty spot to add one."],["Thick solid line with small dots on it","Your line at angle θ. The small dots are where each point lands on it: the scores."],["Thin orange segments","The distance lost: the perpendicular from each point to your line. Shown when \"Show distances lost (projection lines)\" is ticked."],["Dashed orange and dashed green lines, labelled PC1 and PC2","The principal axes from the eigen-decomposition. Their half-length is 2√λ, two standard deviations along each."],["Right plot: solid blue curve","Variance kept as a function of the angle. It peaks at PC1 and bottoms out at PC2, 90° later."],["Right plot: dashed orange curve and the dotted line on top","Distance lost (the mirror image of the blue curve) and the total variance, which does not depend on the angle."],["Round dot on the blue curve, orange square on the dashed curve","Your current angle: the dot marks the kept variance and the square the lost variance at that angle."],["Readouts: Variance along your line, Share of total variance","The variance of the scores on your line, and that value divided by the total."],["Readouts: Squared distance lost (÷ n−1), Kept + lost","The lost variance measured directly from the orange segments, and the sum of the two. It equals the total at every angle."],["Readouts: PC1 direction, Your angle vs PC1","The angle of PC1, and how far your line is from it. It turns green when you are within 3°."],["Readouts: λ₁ from covariance eigen, s₁² / (n−1) from SVD, Difference","The same number computed by two routes. The difference is rounding error."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Keep <i>Cloud: Correlated cloud</i>. Drag <i>Angle θ</i> slowly from 0° to 180° and watch \"Variance along your line\" and the curve on the right.","see":"The variance starts at 2.04 (0°), rises to a peak of 2.63 near 29° (share 93.8%), falls to a trough of 0.17 near 119°, and is back at 2.04 at 180°. \"Your angle vs PC1\" turns green near the peak.","why":"The kept variance is a wave between the two eigenvalues, λ₂ = 0.17 and λ₁ = 2.63, and it repeats every 180° because a line at 0° and a line at 180° are the same line. The peak is PC1 (29.4°); the trough is PC2, 90° later."},{"do":"Set the angle to 90°. Read \"Variance along your line\", \"Squared distance lost\" and \"Kept + lost\". Then try 40° and 130°.","see":"At 90°: kept 0.765, lost 2.036, and \"Kept + lost\" reads 2.801 = total 2.801. At 40° and 130° the two parts change but still add to 2.801.","why":"For every point, (along)² + (across)² is its squared distance from the centre, whatever the line. The readout \"lost\" is measured from the segments and \"kept\" from the projections, so it is a real check, not a copy."},{"do":"Press <i>Snap to PC1</i> and compare the readouts with \"λ₁ from covariance eigen\". Keep \"Show PC1 and PC2 axes\" ticked to see where the line sits.","see":"\"Variance along your line\" = 2.628 = λ₁ (2.627603). \"Squared distance lost\" = 0.173, the other eigenvalue λ₂. \"Your angle vs PC1\" is 0.0° and green. Your line lies on the dashed PC1 axis.","why":"The eigenvalue is not an abstract number: it is exactly the variance of the scores along its eigenvector. In 2-D what the line loses is what PC2 keeps."},{"do":"Choose <i>Cloud: Round cloud (no preferred direction)</i> and press <i>Sweep 0→180°</i>. Reload the page first, so the sample numbering starts again. Then press <i>New sample</i> three times and read \"PC1 direction\" each time.","see":"The curve is almost flat: variance stays between 0.99 and 1.16, so any line keeps 46% to 54% of the total. PC1 direction is 153.8° at first, then 68.8°, 4.0° and 85.8°. On <i>Correlated cloud</i> the same three presses give 35.3°, 31.7° and 33.5°, starting from 29.4°.","why":"With λ₁ ≈ λ₂ no direction is special, so which one is \"largest\" is decided by sampling noise. A PC1 from such a cloud tells you nothing. A stable PC1 needs λ₁ well above λ₂."},{"do":"Start from the round cloud again. Click five times near the top-right corner of the plot (around x = 3.5, y = 2.5).","see":"PC1 turns towards your new points, to about 30°, and its share of the variance rises from about 54% to about 65%.","why":"Variance squares the distance from the centre. Five points about 4.3 away each add roughly 18 to the sum of squares, far more than a typical point (about 2), so a few outliers can steer PC1."},{"do":"Drag several points to new places, watching the last three readouts.","see":"\"λ₁ from covariance eigen\" and \"s₁² / (n−1) from SVD\" stay equal to six decimals, and \"Difference\" stays near 1e-15, or even prints 0.","why":"Both are the same quantity: the eigenvalues of C equal the squared singular values of the centred data divided by n − 1. The tiny difference is floating-point rounding."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Shadows on a line. PC1 is the line where the points' shadows spread out the most. What the shadow hides is the distance from each point to the line."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>kept + lost = total</b>, at every angle (Pythagoras). So \"maximise the spread\" and \"minimise the leftover distance\" are one instruction. In the widget, \"Kept + lost\" never moves."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"An eigenvalue is the variance along its direction, and the eigenvalues add up to the total variance (a + c in 2-D). The share of PC j is λⱼ / Σλ."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"PC1 and PC2 are exactly 90° apart: on the right plot, the trough is 90° after the peak. If λ₁ and λ₂ are close, do not read meaning into PC1."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"The sign of a PC is arbitrary: PC1 and −PC1 are the same line. When two programs disagree on signs, flip one. Compare angles modulo 180° or absolute values."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>np.linalg.eigh(C)</code>: eigenvectors are <b>columns</b>, smallest eigenvalue first. <code>np.linalg.svd(Xc)</code>: PCs are <b>rows</b> of Vt, and <code>s**2/(n-1)</code> are the eigenvalues. <code>PCA().fit(X)</code>: <code>components_</code> (rows) and <code>explained_variance_</code>."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"PC1 is the line of best fit.\"","right":"Regression minimises the <b>vertical</b> error, so only y is allowed to be wrong. PC1 minimises the error measured at <b>right angles</b> to the line, so both columns count equally. In the worked example the slopes differ: regression 0.4 (21.8°), PC1 0.5 (26.6°)."},{"wrong":"\"PC1 is the most important original column.\"","right":"It is a blend of all columns: PC1 = 0.894·income + 0.447·spend (on centred data). Each PC is a weighted mix, with weights given by its eigenvector."},{"wrong":"\"The eigenvalue is the length of the PC arrow.\"","right":"Every PC arrow has length 1. The eigenvalue is the variance of the data along it, a property of the data, not of the arrow."},{"wrong":"\"The direction of most variance is the direction that separates my classes.\"","right":"PCA never sees labels. If classes differ along a low-variance direction, PC1 misses the difference. Whether the top directions help a classifier has to be tested (see the pipeline simulation)."}],"code":[{"title":"Covariance matrix and eigenvectors by hand","py":"import numpy as np\n\nX = np.array([[18, 9], [19, 9], [20, 11], [21, 11], [22, 10]])  # income, spend (Rs thousand)\nXc = X - X.mean(axis=0)               # centre first: PCA looks at spread around the mean\nC = Xc.T @ Xc / (len(X) - 1)          # 2x2 covariance matrix\nvals, vecs = np.linalg.eigh(C)        # eigh = eigen for symmetric matrices, smallest first\n\nprint(\"covariance:\", C.tolist())\nprint(\"eigenvalues:\", vals[::-1].round(3))\nprint(\"PC1:\", np.abs(vecs[:, -1]).round(3), \"share:\", round(vals[-1] / vals.sum(), 3))","out":"covariance: [[2.5, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0]]\neigenvalues: [3.  0.5]\nPC1: [0.894 0.447] share: 0.857","walk":[["X - X.mean(axis=0)","Subtract each column's mean from every row. <code>axis=0</code> means \"average down the rows\", which gives one mean per column. This is the centring step."],["Xc.T @ Xc / (len(X) - 1)","<code>.T</code> swaps rows and columns and <code>@</code> is matrix multiplication. The product is a 2×2 table of sums of products. Dividing by n − 1 makes it the covariance matrix C: variances on the diagonal, covariance off it."],["np.linalg.eigh(C)","Eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a <i>symmetric</i> matrix (the <code>h</code> says so). Eigenvalues come smallest first and the eigenvectors come as <b>columns</b>."],["vals[::-1]","Reverse the list so the largest eigenvalue is first, the order in which PCA lists components."],["vecs[:, -1]","Every row of the last column: the eigenvector of the largest eigenvalue, which is PC1. <code>np.abs</code> hides its arbitrary sign."],["vals[-1] / vals.sum()","The largest eigenvalue over the total: the share of variance PC1 keeps (3.0 / 3.5)."]],"tip":"Use <code>eigh</code>, not <code>eig</code>, for covariance matrices. It is faster, returns real numbers, and sorts them."},{"title":"The SVD route and the variance along a line","py":"import numpy as np\n\nX = np.array([[18, 9], [19, 9], [20, 11], [21, 11], [22, 10]])\nXc = X - X.mean(axis=0)\nU, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(Xc, full_matrices=False)   # Xc = U @ diag(s) @ Vt\n\nprint(\"s**2 / (n-1):\", (s**2 / (len(X) - 1)).round(3))   # same numbers as the eigenvalues\nprint(\"PC1 = first row of Vt:\", np.abs(Vt[0]).round(3))\ntheta = np.arctan(0.5)                               # PC1 angle, about 26.6 degrees\nu = np.array([np.cos(theta), np.sin(theta)])         # unit arrow along that angle\nprint(\"variance along u:\", round(float(np.var(Xc @ u, ddof=1)), 3))","out":"s**2 / (n-1): [3.  0.5]\nPC1 = first row of Vt: [0.894 0.447]\nvariance along u: 3.0","walk":[["np.linalg.svd(Xc, full_matrices=False)","Splits the centred data into <code>U</code>, <code>s</code> and <code>Vt</code>. <code>full_matrices=False</code> asks for the compact form, with as many columns as the data has features."],["s**2 / (len(X) - 1)","The singular values in <code>s</code> are the square roots of (n − 1) times the eigenvalues. Squaring and dividing by n − 1 gives back the variances 3.0 and 0.5."],["Vt[0]","The first <b>row</b> of Vᵀ is PC1. Rows here, columns in <code>eigh</code>: a common source of bugs."],["np.arctan(0.5)","The angle, in radians, of the direction (2, 1): rise 1 over run 2."],["Xc @ u","The scores: the dot product of every centred customer with the unit arrow u."],["np.var(..., ddof=1)","<code>ddof=1</code> divides by n − 1, matching the covariance matrix. The default divides by n and would print 2.4."]],"tip":"This is what scikit-learn does inside <code>PCA</code>. You never have to build C yourself."},{"title":"The same thing with scikit-learn","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = np.array([[18, 9], [19, 9], [20, 11], [21, 11], [22, 10]])\npca = PCA(n_components=2).fit(X)          # fit subtracts the mean for you\n\nprint(pca.explained_variance_.round(3), pca.explained_variance_ratio_.round(3))\nprint(\"PC1 direction:\", np.abs(pca.components_[0]).round(3))\nscores = pca.transform(X)                 # one row per customer, one column per PC\nprint(\"variance of PC1 scores:\", round(float(scores[:, 0].var(ddof=1)), 3))","out":"[3.  0.5] [0.857 0.143]\nPC1 direction: [0.894 0.447]\nvariance of PC1 scores: 3.0","walk":[["PCA(n_components=2)","Creates the model. Nothing is learned yet. <code>n_components</code> is how many PCs to keep."],[".fit(X)","Learns the PCs from X. It subtracts the column means itself, so pass the raw table. The result keeps the mean inside the object."],["explained_variance_","The eigenvalues λ, largest first. A trailing underscore marks something learned during <code>fit</code>."],["explained_variance_ratio_","Each λ divided by the total: 3.0 / 3.5 = 0.857 and 0.5 / 3.5 = 0.143."],["components_[0]","PC1 as a row with one weight per original column. Rows of <code>components_</code> are the PCs."],["pca.transform(X)","The scores: one row per customer, one column per PC. Column 0 is the PC1 score."]],"tip":"The last line closes the loop: the eigenvalue <i>is</i> the variance of the PC1 scores."}],"check":[{"q":"In the simulation the total variance is 3.5. You rotate the line so that \"Variance along your line\" rises from 1.0 to 3.0. What happens to \"Squared distance lost\"?","options":["It falls from 3.5 to 0.5","It falls from 2.5 to 0.5","It stays at 3.5","It rises from 1.0 to 3.0"],"answer":1,"why":"Kept + lost = total at every angle. Kept goes 1.0 to 3.0, so lost goes 3.5 − 1.0 = 2.5 to 3.5 − 3.0 = 0.5."},{"q":"<code>np.linalg.eigh(C)</code> returns the eigenvalues <code>[0.5, 3.0]</code>. Which eigenvector column is PC1, and what share of the variance does it keep?","options":["The first column, 0.5 / 3.5 = 14.3%","Either column, they are equal","The last column, 3.0 / 3.5 = 85.7%","The last column, 3.0 / 0.5 = 600%"],"answer":2,"why":"<code>eigh</code> sorts eigenvalues from smallest to largest, so PC1 is the <b>last</b> column. Its share is its eigenvalue over the sum of all eigenvalues: 3.0 / (0.5 + 3.0)."},{"q":"You choose <i>Round cloud</i> and press <i>New sample</i> several times. What does the \"PC1 direction\" readout do?","options":["It stays at about 0°","It always lands near 45°","It follows the last point you dragged","It jumps to very different angles"],"answer":3,"why":"In a round cloud λ₁ ≈ λ₂, so no direction is special and the winning angle is set by sampling noise. The page shows 153.8°, 68.8°, 4.0° and 85.8° for the first four samples."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why the line that keeps the most variance is also the line the points are closest to.","model":"For each point, (distance along the line)² + (distance from the line)² is the same whatever line you pick, because together they are the point's squared distance from the centre. So the line that makes the \"along\" part largest automatically makes the \"from the line\" part smallest, and that line is the top eigenvector of the covariance matrix."},"cheat":"PC1 is the unit direction u that maximises the variance of the scores u·x. Equivalently it minimises the squared distance left over, because kept + lost = total. It is the eigenvector of the covariance matrix with the largest eigenvalue λ₁, which equals the variance along it. The SVD of centred data gives the same answer: λ = s²/(n−1). Share = λ / Σλ."},"pca/scaling":{"idea":"PCA chases variance, and variance depends on the unit a column is written in. A column with big numbers (proline, in the hundreds) wins PC1 whatever it means. <b>Standardise</b> first, so every column starts with variance 1.","why":"On the raw wine data, PC1 is 99.8% one chemical, proline, only because proline is measured in hundreds and most other columns near 1. The 2-D map then mixes the three cultivars: 67% of a wine's nearest neighbours share its cultivar, against 93% after scaling. Write milligrams as grams and the \"answer\" changes, though no information did.","analogy":"Two judges mark a contest, one out of 10 and the other out of 1000. Add the raw marks and only the second judge matters. A fair total first puts both on the same scale, for example \"how many standard deviations above average is this contestant\". Standardising does that to every column before PCA.","vocab":[["Standardise (z-score)","Replace each value by <span class=\"eq\">z = (x − mean) / sd</span>. The column then has mean 0 and standard deviation 1."],["Standard deviation (sd)","The square root of the variance, in the same unit as the column. Wine: proline 314, magnesium 14.2, hue 0.23."],["Unit change","Writing the same measurement in another unit multiplies the whole column by a constant m (mg to g is m = 0.001). Its variance is multiplied by <b>m²</b>."],["Loading","A feature's weight in a PC (an entry of its eigenvector). A loading near ±1 means the PC is almost that one feature."],["Correlation matrix","The covariance matrix of standardised columns. Its diagonal is all 1s, so each column brings exactly 1 unit of variance."],["Neighbour purity","In the 2-D PC1–PC2 map, the share of each wine's 10 nearest neighbours that belong to the same cultivar. Guessing gives about 34%; 100% means the cultivars form clean clumps."],["Pipeline","A chain such as scale, then PCA, then model, fitted as one object so that each step learns only from training rows."]],"steps":[{"h":"What PCA actually maximises","p":"PCA picks the direction with the largest variance. The variance of a column is the average squared distance from its mean, so it carries the column's <b>unit squared</b>. PCA has no way to know that 314 in proline means something different from 314 in another column. It sees numbers, not meanings."},{"h":"Two columns, two scales","p":"Take age (years) and monthly income (₹). Age varies by about 4 years, income by about ₹4,000. The variance of age is 17 and the variance of income is 17,000,000. Any direction that includes income carries a million times more variance, so PC1 becomes \"income\" and age gets no say. Nobody decided income is more important. The unit did."},{"h":"Wine shows the same thing","p":"Standard deviations in the wine data: proline 314, magnesium 14.2, alcalinity of ash 3.3, colour intensity 2.3, and nine others below 1.2. Proline alone holds 99.8% of the total variance. PC1 therefore explains 99.8% and is almost exactly \"proline\" (loading 1.000). The other twelve columns are nearly invisible."},{"h":"The fix: standardise first","p":"For each column subtract its mean and divide by its standard deviation. Now every column has variance 1, so the total variance equals the number of columns (13) and no column wins by unit. The covariance matrix of the z-scores is the <b>correlation matrix</b>: PCA now looks at which columns move together, not at how big their numbers are."},{"h":"What PC1 becomes","p":"After scaling, PC1 explains 36.2% and many columns contribute: flavanoids 0.42, total phenols 0.40, OD280 0.38, proanthocyanins 0.31, nonflavanoid phenols −0.30. These columns are correlated, so they add up in one direction. PC1 is now a \"phenol-rich or phenol-poor wine\" axis, and the cultivars line up along it."},{"h":"Scaling makes PCA unit-proof","p":"Multiply a column by any constant m, then standardise: <span class=\"eq\">(m·x − m·mean) / (m·sd)</span> is the same z as before. So the standardised PCA gives identical results in grams or milligrams, while raw PCA changes. This is why the unit multiplier does nothing when Standardise is on."},{"h":"When not to standardise","p":"If all columns share one unit and their relative size is meaningful, leave them alone. The 64 pixels of a digit image are all grey levels from 0 to 16. Standardising would boost faint, noisy border pixels to the same weight as the stroke pixels, and three pixels are always 0, so dividing by their sd of 0 fails. Choose on purpose, not by habit."},{"h":"Learn the scaling from training data only","p":"The mean and sd are numbers learned from data. If you standardise the whole table once and then cross-validate, the validation rows have influenced the scaler. Put <code>StandardScaler</code> and <code>PCA</code> in one pipeline, so each is fitted on the training rows of each fold. The pipeline simulation shows this."}],"example":{"title":"five customers, raw versus standardised PCA","setup":"Age (years) and monthly income (₹) of five customers. The two columns rise together (correlation 0.88). Run PCA twice by hand: on the raw numbers, then after standardising.","table":{"head":["Customer","Age","Income (₹)","Age − 30","Income − 30,000"],"rows":[["A","25","27,000","−5","−3,000"],["B","27","25,000","−3","−5,000"],["C","30","30,000","0","0"],["D","33","35,000","3","5,000"],["E","35","33,000","5","3,000"]]},"walk":["<b>Means</b> are 30 years and ₹30,000, which gives the two deviation columns in the table.","<b>Raw covariance</b> (divide by n − 1 = 4). var(age) = (25+9+0+9+25)/4 = 17. var(income) = (9+25+0+25+9) × 10⁶ / 4 = 17,000,000. cov = (15+15+0+15+15) × 1000 / 4 = 15,000.","<b>Raw PCA.</b> C = [[17, 15,000], [15,000, 17,000,000]]. The income entry is a million times the age entry, so λ₁ ≈ 17,000,013 and λ₂ ≈ 3.8 (they multiply to det C = 64,000,000). PC1 = (0.0009, 1.0000): the income axis. PC1 share = <b>99.99998%</b>. Age has no say.","<b>Standardise.</b> sd(age) = √17 = 4.123 and sd(income) = √17,000,000 = 4,123. The covariance of the z-scores is the correlation r = 15,000 / (4.123 × 4,123) = 15,000 / 17,000 = <b>0.882</b>. The correlation matrix is [[1, 0.882], [0.882, 1]].","<b>Standardised PCA.</b> The eigenvalues of [[1, r], [r, 1]] are 1 + r and 1 − r: <b>1.882</b> and <b>0.118</b> (they add to 2, the number of columns). The eigenvector of 1 + r is (1, 1)/√2 = <b>(0.707, 0.707)</b> for any positive r. PC1 share = 1.882 / 2 = <b>94.1%</b>.","<b>Change the unit instead.</b> Write income in ₹ thousand. Now sd(income) = 4.123 = sd(age), and the raw covariance is [[17, 15], [15, 17]], with λ = 17 ± 15 = 32 and 2. PC1 = (0.707, 0.707) and share = 32/34 = 94.1%. The same answer as the standardised one."],"result":"Raw, income in ₹: PC1 is income alone, 99.99998% of the variance. Standardised (or income in ₹ thousand, where both sds are equal): PC1 = (0.707, 0.707), 94.1%. The only thing that changed between the two answers was a unit."},"read":[["Left plot: bars \"Spread of each feature (standard deviation)\"","Each bar is the sd of one wine feature on a <b>log</b> scale: every gridline is 10 times the last. The dashed line is sd = 1. This is what PCA sees. The orange bar marks the feature that has taken over PC1 (only when Standardise is off and PC1 explains over 90%)."],["Middle plot: \"PC1 loadings\"","The weight of each feature in PC1. Blue is positive, orange negative. A bar reaching ±1 means PC1 is nearly that one feature."],["Right plot: markers","The 178 wines on PC1 and PC2. Blue circle: cultivar 1. Orange triangle: cultivar 2. Green square: cultivar 3. The axis ranges rescale with your settings."],["Switch: Standardise features first?","<i>Off (raw units)</i> gives PCA the numbers as measured. <i>On (mean 0, sd 1)</i> standardises every column first."],["Unit lab: Change the unit of, Unit multiplier","Pick a feature and multiply its column by 0.001 to 1000 (log slider). A multiplier of 1000 is the same as reporting it in a unit 1000 times smaller. Each feature remembers its own multiplier. <i>Reset all units</i> sets them back to 1."],["Readouts: PC1 explains, PC2 explains","The share of the total variance carried by each of the first two components."],["Readout: Largest PC1 loading","The feature with the biggest absolute loading, and its size. It turns amber above 0.9: one column is running PC1."],["Readout: Neighbour purity in PC1–PC2 map","The share of each wine's 10 nearest neighbours (in the 2-D map) that share its cultivar. Green above 85%, amber below 75%."],["Hovering over a bar or a dot","Shows the exact sd, loading, or the wine number and cultivar."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the \"Predict first\" question, then leave <i>Standardise features first?</i> on <i>Off (raw units)</i> and read the three charts and four readouts. Hover over the proline bar.","see":"PC1 explains 99.8% and PC2 only 0.2%. The largest loading is proline (1.00). The orange bar in the left plot is proline, with sd 314, about 22 times magnesium (14.2). Neighbour purity is 67%.","why":"Proline's variance is about 99,200 out of a total of about 99,400. PCA maximises variance, so PC1 is nearly the proline axis. The map is a one-feature sort, which does not separate cultivars 2 and 3 well."},{"do":"Keep Standardise <i>Off</i>. With <i>Change the unit of: proline</i>, slide <i>Unit multiplier</i> slowly from × 1 down to × 0.001.","see":"PC1 explains 99.8% at first, falls to a dip of about 67% near × 0.045, then climbs again to 90.9% at × 0.001. The orange bar vanishes while PC1 is below 90% and comes back on <b>magnesium</b> below about × 0.005. At × 0.001 the largest loading is magnesium (1.00) and purity is 57%.","why":"Variance scales with the square of the multiplier, so proline's sd shrinks from 314 towards 0.3. The takeover happens where proline's sd equals magnesium's: 314 × m = 14.2, so m ≈ 0.045. At that point two columns tie and PC1 is a mix of both. No wine changed."},{"do":"Press <i>Reset all units</i>. Choose <i>Change the unit of: alcohol</i> and slide the multiplier up to × 1000.","see":"PC1 explains 92.8% and PC2 7.2%. The largest loading is alcohol (0.96). Purity changes to 75%.","why":"Alcohol's sd grows from 0.81 to 810, which beats proline's 314. The purity moved from 67% to 75% with no new information. It only reflects which two columns the units happen to put on top."},{"do":"Press <i>Reset all units</i>, then switch Standardise to <i>On</i>. Now drag the multiplier of any feature, for example alcohol × 1000 or proline × 0.001.","see":"PC1 explains 36.2% and PC2 19.2%, the largest loading is flavanoids (0.42), purity is 93%, and every readout stays exactly the same however far you drag the multiplier. All bars in the left plot sit at sd = 1.","why":"The z-score cancels the multiplier: (m·x − m·mean) / (m·sd) = (x − mean) / sd. Standardised PCA cannot be moved by a change of unit."},{"do":"With Standardise <i>On</i>, study the loadings chart and the right plot.","see":"Many bars of different heights: flavanoids 0.42, total phenols 0.40 and OD280/OD315 0.38 positive; nonflavanoid phenols −0.30 and malic acid −0.25 negative. In the map, cultivar 1 (blue circles) is on the right, cultivar 3 (green squares) on the left and cultivar 2 (orange triangles) in the middle and lower.","why":"Flavanoids and total phenols are strongly correlated (r = 0.87), so they add up in one direction. PC1 reads \"phenol-rich versus phenol-poor wine\", and the cultivars differ along it: mean PC1 score +2.3, 0.0 and −2.7 for cultivars 1, 2 and 3."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Variance has the unit <b>squared</b>. Multiply a column by m and its variance is multiplied by m². Milligrams to grams (m = 0.001) divides the variance by a million."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Before trusting a PCA, look at the sd of each column and the largest loading of PC1. One sd 10 times bigger than the rest, or one loading near ±1, means a unit is speaking, not the data. The page turns that readout amber for you."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Different units (rupees, years, mg per litre): standardise. One shared unit whose size matters (pixels, same sensor): think first, often leave it raw."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"z = (x − mean) / sd. After it every column has mean 0 and variance 1, so the total variance is the number of columns (13 for wine) and each PC's share is its eigenvalue divided by 13."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"The log-scale bar chart of sd is the quickest diagnostic. Bars about equal: scaling will change little. One bar sticking out: that column will take PC1."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=2))</code>. The scaler learns its means and sds from the rows you <code>fit</code> on, which in cross-validation are the training rows only."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"PC1 is proline, so proline is the most important wine feature.\"","right":"It means proline has the biggest numbers. Variance in raw units mostly reflects the unit, not importance. Write proline in grams instead of milligrams and magnesium takes PC1."},{"wrong":"\"Standardising throws information away.\"","right":"It shifts and rescales each column by constants, and you can undo it exactly. What it removes is the unit's say in the result."},{"wrong":"\"Standardise always, for every dataset.\"","right":"It is the right default for mixed units. For columns that share a meaningful unit, such as the 64 grey-level pixels of a digit, standardising lifts faint noisy pixels to the same weight as strokes, and three constant pixels (sd 0) cannot be divided by."},{"wrong":"\"Scale the whole table once, then split into folds.\"","right":"Mean and sd are learned from data. Learn them from the training rows only, inside a pipeline. Otherwise the validation rows have already influenced the scaler (see the pipeline simulation)."}],"code":[{"title":"Raw versus standardised PCA on the wine data","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\ndata = load_wine()\nX = data.data\nXs = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)        # every column: mean 0, sd 1\nfor name, M in ((\"raw\", X), (\"standardised\", Xs)):\n    pca = PCA().fit(M)\n    top = abs(pca.components_[0]).argmax()    # feature with the largest |loading| on PC1\n    print(name, \"PC1 share:\", round(pca.explained_variance_ratio_[0], 3), \"| top feature:\", data.feature_names[top])","out":"raw PC1 share: 0.998 | top feature: proline\nstandardised PC1 share: 0.362 | top feature: flavanoids","walk":[["load_wine()","Returns a bundle: <code>.data</code> is the 178 × 13 table and <code>.feature_names</code> the column names, in the same order."],["StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)","<code>fit</code> learns each column's mean and sd, <code>transform</code> applies <code>(x − mean) / sd</code>. <code>fit_transform</code> does both in one call."],["PCA().fit(M)","With no <code>n_components</code>, PCA keeps all 13 components, ranked by variance."],["pca.components_[0]","PC1, a row of 13 loadings, one per feature."],["abs(...).argmax()","Absolute values first (a loading of −0.9 is as big as +0.9), then the index of the largest. It is also the position in <code>feature_names</code>."],["explained_variance_ratio_[0]","The share of total variance of the first component."]],"tip":"Here the scaler is fitted on all rows, which is fine for exploring. For model scoring put it inside a pipeline."},{"title":"Change a unit and watch PC1 change","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\ndata = load_wine()\nX = data.data.copy()\nX[:, 12] *= 0.001                             # proline in grams instead of milligrams\nfor name, M in ((\"raw\", X), (\"standardised\", StandardScaler().fit_transform(X))):\n    pca = PCA().fit(M)\n    print(name, round(pca.explained_variance_ratio_[0], 3), data.feature_names[abs(pca.components_[0]).argmax()])","out":"raw 0.909 magnesium\nstandardised 0.362 flavanoids","walk":[[".copy()","Make a separate copy so that the next line does not change the original table."],["X[:, 12] *= 0.001","Column 12 (all rows) is proline. <code>*=</code> multiplies in place, exactly what the unit multiplier does on the page."],["raw 0.909 magnesium","PC1 now explains 90.9% and belongs to magnesium. The page shows the same at × 0.001."],["standardised 0.362 flavanoids","Identical to the previous snippet: the unit change was cancelled by the scaler."]],"tip":"This is the page's Unit lab in four lines. Try 0.1, 0.01 and 1000 for other columns."},{"title":"Scaling and PCA as one pipeline","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nX, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)\npipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=2))\nZ = pipe.fit_transform(X)                     # scale, then project, in one call\nprint(\"map shape:\", Z.shape)\nprint(\"proline mean and sd learned by the scaler:\", round(pipe[0].mean_[12], 1), round(pipe[0].scale_[12], 1))\nprint(\"PCA shares:\", pipe.named_steps[\"pca\"].explained_variance_ratio_.round(3))","out":"map shape: (178, 2)\nproline mean and sd learned by the scaler: 746.9 314.0\nPCA shares: [0.362 0.192]","walk":[["load_wine(return_X_y=True)","Returns just the features <code>X</code> and the labels <code>y</code> (0, 1, 2 for the three cultivars)."],["make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=2))","Chains the steps in order. Each step's output is the next step's input, and names are made from the class names (<code>standardscaler</code>, <code>pca</code>)."],["pipe.fit_transform(X)","Fits the scaler, transforms, fits PCA on that, and transforms again. The result has 178 rows and 2 columns: the map on the page."],["pipe[0].mean_, .scale_","<code>pipe[0]</code> is the first step, the scaler. Its <code>mean_</code> and <code>scale_</code> (the sd) hold what it learned."],["pipe.named_steps[\"pca\"]","Another way to reach a step, by its name."]],"tip":"StandardScaler divides by n, PCA by n − 1, so scikit-learn's eigenvalues here add to 13.07, not 13. The shares and directions are unaffected."}],"check":[{"q":"Why does PC1 of the <b>raw</b> wine data point almost entirely at proline?","options":["Proline has by far the largest variance, because of the units it is measured in","Proline separates the cultivars better than any other feature","Proline is correlated with all the other features","PCA always picks the last column"],"answer":0,"why":"PCA looks for the direction of largest variance. Proline's sd is 314, against 14 for magnesium and about 1 or less for most others, so it holds 99.8% of the variance. That says nothing about how useful proline is."},{"q":"A feature has variance 4 when measured in metres. What is its variance in centimetres?","options":["40,000","200","400","4"],"answer":0,"why":"Centimetre values are 100 times larger, and variance squares the scale: 4 × 100² = 40,000."},{"q":"Standardise is <b>On</b> and you multiply alcohol by 1000. What happens to \"PC1 explains\"?","options":["It becomes 100%","It falls to 0%","It stays at 36.2%","It jumps to about 93%"],"answer":2,"why":"The z-score divides the multiplier out again: (1000·x − 1000·mean) / (1000·sd) = (x − mean) / sd. The standardised data, and therefore every PC, is unchanged."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why PCA on unscaled data can be dominated by one column, and what to do about it.","model":"PCA looks for the direction with the most variance, and variance grows with the size of the units, so a column measured in hundreds (like proline) swamps columns measured near 1 even though it is no more important. Standardising every column to mean 0 and sd 1 first, inside a pipeline fitted on training rows, removes the unit's influence so PCA reflects how the columns move together."},"cheat":"PCA maximises variance, and variance carries the unit squared (a column times m has variance times m²). Raw wine: proline (sd 314) takes 99.8% of PC1, purity 67%. Standardise with z = (x − mean)/sd, so every column has variance 1 and PCA uses the correlation matrix: PC1 36.2%, purity 93%. Skip it only for columns sharing a meaningful unit."},"pca/choosing-k":{"idea":"PCA ranks directions by the variance they carry. To compress, keep the first k and rebuild each image from k numbers. The cumulative-variance curve prices every k, but no single k is \"correct\".","why":"A digit image is 64 numbers, yet pixels move together, so fewer numbers might do. With too few components, strokes blur and digits look alike. With too many, you store and model noise for nothing. You need a number that says what each k keeps, not a guess.","analogy":"Describing a friend to someone who has never met them. \"Tall, dark hair, glasses\" gives a recognisable sketch. Ten more facts sharpen it. The next hundred add freckles. The first facts carry the most, each extra fact adds less, and you stop when the picture is good enough for your purpose. Components are the facts, ranked by how much they add.","vocab":[["Explained variance ratio","The share of the total variance carried by one component, <span class=\"eq\">λⱼ / Σλ</span>. For the digits, PC1 has 14.9%."],["Cumulative variance","The running total of those shares for components 1 to k. It is the variance that k components keep."],["Scree plot","A bar chart of the share of each component, largest first. People look for an \"elbow\" where the bars suddenly get small."],["Score","How strongly an image uses component j: <span class=\"eq\">sⱼ = vⱼ · (x − mean)</span>. An image is stored as k scores."],["Reconstruction","Rebuilding an image from its scores: <span class=\"eq\">x̂ = mean + s₁v₁ + … + s_k v_k</span>."],["Variance target","A rule that picks the smallest k whose cumulative variance reaches a chosen level, such as 95%."],["Reconstruction error","The sum of squared differences between original and rebuilt pixels. As a share of the total spread around the mean image, it equals 1 − cumulative variance."]],"steps":[{"h":"Where we start","p":"Each digit is an 8×8 grey-level image: 64 numbers from 0 to 16. Neighbouring pixels move together, because a stroke lights several at once. So the 64 numbers are far from independent, and fewer numbers might describe an image almost as well. The question is how many, and PCA provides a price list."},{"h":"Components ranked by variance","p":"PCA on the 1,797 images gives 64 directions, each an 8×8 \"component image\", ranked by variance: λ₁ ≥ λ₂ ≥ … The total variance, 1202.15 (the sum of the 64 pixel variances), is split exactly among them. The share of component j is <span class=\"eq\">λⱼ / 1202.15</span>: PC1 14.9%, PC2 13.6%, PC3 11.8%, PC4 8.4%, PC5 5.8%."},{"h":"The scree plot: look for an elbow","p":"A scree plot is a bar chart of those shares. If the bars fell steeply and then flattened, the bend (the \"elbow\") would suggest k. For the digits they fall gradually: 14.9, 13.6, 11.8, 8.4, 5.8, 4.9, 4.3, 3.7 … There is no clear elbow, so the scree plot alone does not tell you k."},{"h":"The cumulative curve is the price list","p":"Add the shares up as you go. Keeping k components keeps <span class=\"eq\">cum(k) = (λ₁ + … + λ_k) / total</span> of the variance. For digits: k = 5 keeps 54.5%, k = 10 keeps 73.8%, k = 21 keeps 90.3%, k = 29 keeps 95.5%, k = 41 keeps 99.0%. A <b>variance target</b> reads the curve backwards: the smallest k whose cum(k) reaches the target."},{"h":"Rebuild an image from k numbers","p":"Store only the k scores of each image. To rebuild, start from the mean image and add each component image weighted by its score: <span class=\"eq\">x̂ = mean + s₁v₁ + … + s_k v_k</span>. More components bring more detail, and at k = 64 the rebuild is exact. The bottom picture on the page shows this for ten digits."},{"h":"The error identity","p":"The squared error left after rebuilding, as a share of the total squared spread around the mean image, equals exactly <b>1 − cum(k)</b>. The dropped directions are at right angles to the kept ones, so by Pythagoras squared lengths just add up, and each dropped direction contributes its λ. At k = 29 the error left is 4.52%."},{"h":"Three ways to choose k","p":"(1) A variance target such as 90, 95 or 99%: quick and common. (2) Cross-validation: treat k as a hyper-parameter of a pipeline and pick the k that scores best on the real task (next simulation). (3) The purpose itself: k = 2 for a plot, or a storage budget. None is a law. The 95% rule is a convention."},{"h":"What the target does not tell you","p":"Variance is not usefulness: a low-variance component can still carry the class signal, and PCA never sees labels. Also, \"k numbers per image\" ignores the shared mean image and component images. For 1,797 images at k = 29 you store 54,033 numbers instead of 115,008, which is 2.1 times smaller, not 2.2. The gap matters more with fewer images."}],"example":{"title":"reading the variance table of the digits","setup":"PCA on the 1,797 digit images gives 64 eigenvalues λ (the variance of each component). They add up to <b>1202.15</b>. Here are the first five, then selected later ones.","table":{"head":["k","λₖ","Share λₖ / 1202.15","Cumulative share","Error left = 1 − cum"],"rows":[["1","179.01","14.89%","14.89%","85.11%"],["2","163.72","13.62%","28.51%","71.49%"],["3","141.79","11.79%","40.30%","59.70%"],["4","101.10","8.41%","48.71%","51.29%"],["5","69.51","5.78%","54.50%","45.50%"],["10","37.01","3.08%","73.82%","26.18%"],["21","10.69","0.89%","90.32%","9.68%"],["28","6.19","0.52%","94.99%","5.01%"],["29","5.88","0.49%","95.48%","4.52%"],["41","2.28","0.19%","99.01%","0.99%"]]},"walk":["<b>Share of one component.</b> PC1: 179.01 / 1202.15 = 0.1489, so 14.89%. PC2: 163.72 / 1202.15 = 13.62%.","<b>Cumulative.</b> Add as you go: 14.89 + 13.62 = 28.51, then + 11.79 = 40.30, + 8.41 = 48.71, + 5.78 = 54.50. Or add the eigenvalues first: (179.01 + 163.72 + 141.79 + 101.10 + 69.51) / 1202.15 = 655.13 / 1202.15 = 54.50%.","<b>Error left.</b> The dropped eigenvalues add to 1202.15 − 655.13 = 547.02, which is 45.50% of the total, exactly 100% − 54.50%. This is the page's \"Squared error left ÷ total\" readout at k = 5.","<b>Target 95%.</b> Find the first k whose cumulative share reaches 95.00%. k = 28 gives 94.99%, a hair short. k = 29 gives <b>95.48%</b>. So k = 29.","<b>Other targets.</b> 90% needs k = 21 (90.32%; k = 20 gives only 89.43%). 99% needs k = 41 (99.01%).","<b>Storage.</b> 29 numbers per image instead of 64: 64 / 29 = 2.2 times smaller, or about 2.1 times once the shared component images are counted."],"result":"To keep 95% of the variance of the digits you need k = 29 of the 64 components, which is 45% of them. Each image is then stored as 29 numbers and rebuilt with 4.52% of the squared error left. The bars of the scree plot alone would not have told you this."},"read":[["Left plot: \"Scree: variance per component\"","Each bar is the percentage of variance of one component (the first 20 are shown). Blue bars are the components you keep, 1 to k. Grey bars are dropped. If k is above 20 a label \"k = N (off the chart)\" appears. Hover for the exact value."],["Middle plot: \"Cumulative variance kept\"","The running total. The dashed horizontal line is your variance target. The dark dot and dashed vertical line mark k."],["Bottom picture, row \"original\"","Ten digits, one of each from 0 to 9. Each small square is a pixel: more ink means a higher grey level."],["Bottom picture, row \"k = N\"","The same ten digits rebuilt from k components: the mean image plus k weighted component images."],["Slider: Components kept, k","Sets k, and moves the target to the variance that k keeps (limited to 50%–99.9%)."],["Slider: Or set a variance target","Moves k to the smallest value that reaches the target. The buttons 90%, 95%, 99% do the same in one click."],["Button: New digits","Chooses ten different digits to look at. It does not change any readout, because the readouts use all 1,797 images."],["Readout: Variance kept (cumulative)","cum(k), the share of variance that k components keep."],["Readouts: 1 − cumulative variance, Squared error left ÷ total, Difference","The first is the prediction. The second is measured: rebuild all 1,797 images with k components, add the squared pixel errors, divide by the total spread around the mean image. \"Difference\" is the gap, which is rounding noise."],["Readout: Numbers stored per image","\"k instead of 64\" and the ratio 64/k. It counts only the scores, not the shared component images."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the \"Predict first\" question. Then press <i>90%</i>, <i>95%</i> and <i>99%</i>. Afterwards drag <i>Or set a variance target</i> to both ends.","see":"k = 21, 29 and 41, with \"Variance kept\" 90.32%, 95.48% and 99.01%, a little over each target. At the ends of the slider: 50% needs k = 5, and 99.9% needs k = 49.","why":"k is a whole number, so the curve usually jumps past the target. The curve is steep at the start and flat at the end: 29 components keep 95.5% but the remaining 35 add only 4.5 points."},{"do":"Set <i>Components kept, k</i> to 1 and look at the bottom row.","see":"The ten rebuilt digits are blurry smudges that look alike: one fixed pattern, shown in two versions (added or subtracted). You cannot read the original digit from them. \"Variance kept\" is 14.89% and \"Numbers stored per image\" says 1 instead of 64.","why":"With k = 1, every image is the mean image plus s₁ times one fixed component image. Only the strength and the sign of s₁ differ from digit to digit, so you get one pattern, positive or negative."},{"do":"Move k through 2, 5, 10, 21 and 29, reading \"Variance kept (cumulative)\" and the bottom row each time.","see":"Variance kept: 28.5%, 54.5%, 73.8%, 90.3%, 95.5%. At k = 10 the ten digits are readable but soft, and at k = 29 they are close to the originals. From k = 1 to 5 you gain 39.6 points. From k = 29 to 64 you gain 4.5 in total.","why":"Eigenvalues shrink quickly: λ₁ = 179.0 but λ₂₉ = 5.9, about 30 times smaller. A late component adds little variance, and little visible detail."},{"do":"Move k to a few values (for example 3, 12, 25, 50) and compare \"1 − cumulative variance\" with \"Squared error left ÷ total\", and look at \"Difference\".","see":"The two percentages agree to every digit shown (at k = 10 both read 26.18%). \"Difference\" is tiny: about 6e-14 at k = 3 and 1e-16 at k = 50, shrinking as k grows.","why":"It is an exact identity, not an approximation. The first number is a prediction from the eigenvalues, the second is measured on the rebuilt pixels, and they differ only by floating-point rounding."},{"do":"Press <i>New digits</i> several times.","see":"Only the ten pictures change. The curves and every readout stay exactly the same.","why":"The readouts are computed over all 1,797 images, not over the ten shown. The ten are a sample to look at, so a digit that is hard to read at your k says nothing about the numbers."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"The cumulative curve is a price list. Read it left to right (\"k numbers keep this much\") or right to left (\"this much needs k numbers\"). It climbs steeply at first and flattens: early components are cheap, late ones are expensive."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>Error left = 1 − cumulative variance.</b> A 95% target means you accept 5% of the squared error. The page's two readouts prove it."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"In code: <code>np.argmax(cum >= target) + 1</code>. <code>argmax</code> finds the first True, and the +1 is because Python counts from 0 while k counts from 1. Forgetting it gives 28 instead of 29, and 94.99% instead of 95.48%."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"The shares must add to 100%, and cum(64) = 100%. Here three pixels are blank in every image, so the last three eigenvalues are 0. Only 61 directions carry any variance, and the curve is exactly 100% from k = 61 on."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Check the task, not only the variance. A logistic regression on the first k components, scored by 5-fold cross-validation, gets 57% at k = 2, 89% at k = 10, 91.0% at k = 29 and 91.3% with all 64 pixels. A 95% target keeps nearly all the accuracy, and 74% (k = 10) loses about 2.5 points."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>PCA(n_components=0.95)</code>: a float below 1 is a variance target, an integer is k itself. <code>pca.n_components_</code> tells you what it chose."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"Keeping 95% of the variance means keeping 95% of the components.\"","right":"Here 95% needs 29 of 64 components (45%). Other data may need 3 of 64, or 60 of 64. It depends on how strongly the columns are correlated."},{"wrong":"\"The elbow of the scree plot gives k.\"","right":"Only if there is an elbow. The digits bars shrink smoothly (14.9, 13.6, 11.8, 8.4, 5.8, 4.9 …), so use the cumulative curve with a target, or cross-validation."},{"wrong":"\"With 95% of the variance kept, I keep 95% of what my model needs.\"","right":"Variance measures how the pixels spread, not what a classifier needs. PCA never sees the labels, so test the whole pipeline with cross-validation."},{"wrong":"\"An error of 4.52% means 4.52% of the pixels are wrong.\"","right":"It is the share of the <b>squared deviation from the mean image</b> that is left over. Every pixel is a little off, but none is wrong in the yes-or-no sense of a misread pixel."}],"code":[{"title":"Variance per component and the k for each target","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data                    # 1797 images, 64 pixels each\npca = PCA().fit(X)                        # keep all 64 components, ranked by variance\ncum = np.cumsum(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)   # running total of the shares\nprint(\"first 5 shares (%):\", (100 * pca.explained_variance_ratio_[:5]).round(2))\nfor target in (0.90, 0.95, 0.99):\n    k = int(np.argmax(cum >= target)) + 1  # first position where the target is reached\n    print(f\"target {target}: k = {k}, kept {cum[k - 1]:.4f}\")","out":"first 5 shares (%): [14.89 13.62 11.79  8.41  5.78]\ntarget 0.9: k = 21, kept 0.9032\ntarget 0.95: k = 29, kept 0.9548\ntarget 0.99: k = 41, kept 0.9901","walk":[["load_digits().data","The 1,797 × 64 table of pixel values. Each row is one 8×8 image flattened into 64 numbers."],["PCA().fit(X)","With no <code>n_components</code>, PCA keeps all 64 components. <code>fit</code> learns them from the images and subtracts the mean image itself."],["explained_variance_ratio_","An array of 64 shares λⱼ / Σλ, largest first, adding up to 1."],["np.cumsum(...)","Running total: element j is the share kept by the first j + 1 components."],["cum >= target","A True/False array that is False until the target is reached and True from then on."],["np.argmax(...) + 1","<code>argmax</code> returns the position of the first True. Positions start at 0 but k starts at 1, hence the +1."],["f\"... {cum[k - 1]:.4f}\"","An f-string: <code>{...}</code> inserts a value and <code>:.4f</code> prints four decimals. <code>cum[k - 1]</code> is the share for k components."]],"tip":"The numbers 21, 29 and 41 are the ones on the page, because the page runs the same PCA on the same images."},{"title":"Let scikit-learn pick k from a variance target","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data\npca = PCA(n_components=0.95).fit(X)       # a float below 1 means \"variance target\"\nZ = pca.transform(X)                      # each image is now k numbers\n\nprint(\"k chosen:\", pca.n_components_)\nprint(\"shape before and after:\", X.shape, Z.shape)\nprint(\"kept:\", round(float(pca.explained_variance_ratio_.sum()), 4))","out":"k chosen: 29\nshape before and after: (1797, 64) (1797, 29)\nkept: 0.9548","walk":[["PCA(n_components=0.95)","A float between 0 and 1 means \"keep just enough components to reach this share of the variance\". An integer would mean \"keep exactly this many\"."],["pca.transform(X)","Computes the scores for all images, shape (1797, 29): one row per image, one column per kept component."],["pca.n_components_","What PCA chose. 29 matches the \"95%\" button on the page."],["X.shape, Z.shape","Shapes read (rows, columns). The rows stay the same, the columns fall from 64 to 29."],["explained_variance_ratio_.sum()","Only the 29 kept shares now, added: 0.9548, the variance the 29 components keep."]],"tip":"In a model pipeline, <code>n_components=0.95</code> is fine for a quick start, but a cross-validated k usually beats a fixed target."},{"title":"Check the error identity yourself","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data\npca = PCA(n_components=10).fit(X)\nXhat = pca.inverse_transform(pca.transform(X))   # compress to 10 numbers, rebuild 64 pixels\n\nleft = ((X - Xhat) ** 2).sum()                   # squared error left, all images\ntotal = ((X - X.mean(axis=0)) ** 2).sum()        # total squared spread around the mean image\nprint(\"error left / total:\", round(float(left / total), 4))\nprint(\"1 - kept variance :\", round(1 - float(pca.explained_variance_ratio_.sum()), 4))","out":"error left / total: 0.2618\n1 - kept variance : 0.2618","walk":[["pca.transform(X)","Compress: each image becomes 10 scores."],["pca.inverse_transform(...)","Expand: mean image + each score times its component image. The result has 64 pixels again, but only 10 numbers of information."],["((X - Xhat) ** 2).sum()","The squared pixel error over all images, added up: the error left."],["((X - X.mean(axis=0)) ** 2).sum()","The total squared spread around the mean image: what PCA started with."],["1 - ...explained_variance_ratio_.sum()","The prediction from the eigenvalues. Both lines print 0.2618: the identity holds."]],"tip":"This is exactly what the two readouts \"1 − cumulative variance\" and \"Squared error left ÷ total\" compare, for every k."}],"check":[{"q":"The digits scree plot falls smoothly, with no elbow. What is a sound way to choose k?","options":["Pick a target on the cumulative curve, or cross-validate a model that uses the components","Use k = 2 because plots are 2-D","Keep the components whose bars are exactly zero","Take k = 1, the tallest bar"],"answer":0,"why":"With no elbow the scree plot cannot decide k. A variance target on the cumulative curve is the quick way. Cross-validating the model that will use the components checks that the chosen k works for the real task."},{"q":"The eigenvalues of a small dataset are [5, 3, 1.5, 0.5]. What is the smallest k that keeps at least 90% of the variance?","options":["2","1","4","3"],"answer":3,"why":"The total is 10. The cumulative shares are 50% (k = 1), 80% (k = 2), 95% (k = 3), 100% (k = 4). 80% is below 90% and 95% is above it, so k = 3."},{"q":"You move k from 29 to 41 on the page. What happens to \"Squared error left ÷ total\"?","options":["It falls to exactly 0%","It rises from 4.5% to 5.5%","It stays at 4.5%","It falls from about 4.5% to about 1.0%"],"answer":3,"why":"Error left = 1 − cumulative variance. At k = 29 the cumulative variance is 95.48%, so 4.52% is left. At k = 41 it is 99.01%, so 0.99% is left. It reaches 0 only when every non-constant direction is kept (k = 61)."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain how you would choose the number of PCA components for the digits, and what \"95% of the variance\" means.","model":"Add up the variance shares of the components from the largest down and keep the smallest k whose total reaches your target, for example 29 components for 95% on the digits. The missing 5% is exactly the share of squared error you have left when you rebuild the images from those k components, but you should still check that your model works well with that k."},"cheat":"The share of component j is λⱼ/Σλ, and cum(k) is the variance that k components keep. Choose the smallest k with cum(k) ≥ target (digits: 21, 29, 41 for 90, 95, 99%) or cross-validate the real task. Rebuild with x̂ = mean + Σⱼ≤k sⱼvⱼ. Squared error left ÷ total = 1 − cum(k), exactly."},"pca/denoising":{"idea":"Noise adds the same wobble to every direction in the data, while a digit's shape lives in a few directions. Keep only the leading PCA directions and rebuild: the shape stays and most of the noise is thrown away.","why":"Images from cheap sensors or poor scans carry random speckle, and a model trained on speckle learns the speckle. Dropping the directions that hold only noise gives cleaner input. Drop too many and you also drop real strokes. The right number of components depends on how noisy the data are.","analogy":"A song on a crackly radio. The tune sits in a few frequency bands, and the crackle is spread equally over all of them. Turn down every band that has less tune than crackle and the sound gets cleaner. Turn down too many and you muffle the tune. Bands are components, tune is signal variance λ, and crackle is the noise variance σ².","vocab":[["Noise level σ","The standard deviation of the random number added to every pixel, in grey levels (the pixel scale is 0 to 16). The noise variance is σ²."],["Mean squared error (MSE)","The average of (rebuilt − clean)² over all pixels of all images. The page plots it against k."],["Signal variance λⱼ","The variance of the <i>clean</i> digits along component j. The first few are large, the later ones tiny."],["Noise floor","Noise adds σ² to the variance in every direction, so the weakest components of noisy data all sit near σ²."],["Reconstruction","Project an image onto the top k components, then map the k scores back to 64 pixels. It is the denoised image."],["Variance target","A rule that sets k to the smallest value that keeps, for example, 50% of the variance of the noisy data. A starting point, not an optimum."],["Fit on noisy, judge on clean","On this page PCA sees only the noisy images. The clean ones are used only to measure the error."]],"steps":[{"h":"The noise model","p":"Each pixel of each image gets its own random number from a bell curve with standard deviation σ: <span class=\"eq\">noisy = clean + σ·z</span>. At σ = 4 on a 0 to 16 scale this is strong. The squared error of the noisy input against the clean image averages σ² = 16 per pixel (the page shows 15.98). The job is to get closer to the clean image than that, using only the noisy ones."},{"h":"Signal is concentrated, noise is spread","p":"The clean digits vary a lot along a few directions and hardly at all along most: λ₁ = 179, λ₁₀ = 37, λ₃₀ = 5.2, λ₅₀ = 0.5. Noise is the same in every direction: it adds σ² = 16 to each of the 64. So along the first 16 directions the digit variance beats the noise, and along the other 48 the noise beats the digit."},{"h":"PCA still finds the digit directions","p":"Independent noise adds σ² times the identity matrix to the covariance matrix: <span class=\"eq\">C_noisy ≈ C_clean + σ²·I</span>. Adding a multiple of the identity raises every eigenvalue by σ² and leaves the eigenvectors alone. So PCA fitted on the noisy images still finds the digit directions at the top. That is why the page can fit PCA on noisy data only."},{"h":"Denoise = keep k directions and rebuild","p":"Project each noisy image onto the top k components, then map back to 64 pixels. In the 64 − k dropped directions, both the signal and the noise are thrown away. In the k kept directions, both stay. The rebuilt image is the denoised one, and its error is measured against the clean image."},{"h":"Two errors that trade off","p":"Compared with the clean image, the error per pixel is about <span class=\"eq\">[ Σⱼ>k λⱼ + k·σ² ] / 64</span>. The first term is the signal you threw away, and it shrinks as k grows. The second is the noise you kept, and it grows by σ²/64 for every component added. A falling term plus a rising term gives a <b>U-shaped curve</b>."},{"h":"Where the bottom of the U is","p":"Adding component j changes the sum by σ² − λⱼ. It helps while λⱼ > σ² and hurts afterwards. So keep the components whose signal variance exceeds the noise variance. At σ = 4, σ² = 16, and λ₁₆ = 16.95 > 16 > λ₁₇ = 15.85, so about 16 components (the page finds 15). More noise means a larger σ² and fewer components."},{"h":"When you do not know σ","p":"Real data rarely come with a σ. You can read it from the flat tail of the scree plot of the noisy data, where the smallest eigenvalues sit near σ², or choose k by testing the downstream task. A fixed variance target ignores σ: at σ = 8 the 50% target asks for 22 components when the best is 4, and its error is 27.8 against 14.2."},{"h":"Limits","p":"PCA denoising is linear. It removes whatever sits in low-variance directions, including real fine detail such as thin strokes. It works because the noise is equal in every direction and the signal is not. If the noise is stronger in some directions, or the signal is spread over many, the gain is smaller."}],"example":{"title":"why about 16 components at σ = 4, by hand","setup":"Noise σ = 4, so the noise variance is σ² = 16. The clean digits have 64 variances λ₁ ≥ λ₂ ≥ … that add up to 1202.15. Keeping component j keeps its signal λⱼ but also lets in 16 of noise. Estimate the error per pixel as (signal thrown away + noise kept) / 64.","table":{"head":["k","λₖ","Signal thrown away, Σⱼ>k λⱼ","Noise kept, k × 16","Estimate (sum ÷ 64)","Page (first noise sample)"],"rows":[["1","179.01","1023.1","16","16.2","16.3"],["5","69.51","547.0","80","9.8","9.9"],["10","37.01","314.7","160","7.4","7.6"],["15","17.64","198.0","240","6.8","7.1"],["16","16.95","181.0","256","6.8","7.2"],["17","15.85","165.2","272","6.8","7.2"],["20","10.89","127.1","320","7.0","7.5"],["30","5.16","49.2","480","8.3","9.0"],["64","0.00","0.0","1024","16.0","16.0"]]},"walk":["<b>Noise energy.</b> σ² = 16 in each of 64 directions, so 64 × 16 = 1024 in all. Per pixel, 1024 / 64 = 16. This is the error of the noisy input, the dashed line (the page shows 15.98).","<b>Keep k components.</b> You throw away the signal in directions k + 1 to 64 and keep the noise in k directions. Estimate = (Σⱼ>k λⱼ + 16·k) / 64.","<b>k = 10.</b> The top ten eigenvalues add to 887.46, so the signal thrown away is 1202.15 − 887.46 = 314.7. Noise kept = 10 × 16 = 160. Estimate = (314.7 + 160) / 64 = <b>7.42</b>.","<b>k = 1.</b> (1023.1 + 16) / 64 = 16.24. That is slightly <i>worse</i> than the noisy input (16.0): one component throws away too much signal.","<b>Where it bottoms out.</b> Going from k − 1 to k changes the sum by 16 − λₖ. λ₁₆ = 16.95 is above 16, so adding it helps. λ₁₇ = 15.85 is below, so adding it hurts. The minimum is at k = 16, estimate 6.83. The page finds k = 15 with 7.15. Its directions come from the noisy images and are slightly off, so its errors run above the estimate (by 0.3 at k = 15 and 0.7 at k = 30).","<b>Double the noise.</b> σ = 8 gives σ² = 64. λ₅ = 69.5 is above 64 and λ₆ = 59.1 is below, so keep about 5 (the page finds 4). A fixed 50% variance target would ask for 22, with error 27.8 against 14.2 at k = 4."],"result":"Keep component j if its signal variance λⱼ is larger than the noise variance σ². That gives about 16 components at σ = 4 and 5 at σ = 8, close to the best k that the page finds (15 and 4). It cuts the error from 16 to about 7 at σ = 4, and from 64 to about 14 at σ = 8."},"read":[["Top picture, three rows of ten digits","Row \"clean\": the original images. Row \"noisy\": clean plus noise of level σ. Row \"k = N\": the noisy digits after PCA with k components. Pixel values are clipped to 0–16 for display only."],["Solid blue curve","The mean squared error against the <b>clean</b> digits if you keep k components, for k from 1 to 64."],["Dashed orange horizontal line","The error of the noisy input itself, about σ². Where the blue curve is below it, PCA has helped."],["Dark dot and dashed vertical line","Your current k."],["Green diamond labelled \"best k = N\"","The lowest point of the blue curve: the k with the least error for this noise sample."],["Slider: Noise level σ (grey levels)","From 0 to 8. The same random noise numbers are scaled up or down, so the noise pattern keeps its shape as you slide."],["Buttons: Variance target 50%, Best k, New noise","The first sets k to the smallest value that keeps 50% of the noisy data's variance. The second jumps to the lowest point. The third draws new random noise and ten new digits."],["Readouts: Error of the noisy input (MSE), Error after PCA at your k, Error removed","Error before, error after at your k, and the share of the error that PCA removed. Negative means it made things worse."],["Readouts: Best k, Variance kept at your k, k for a 50% variance target","The best k and its error, the share of the <i>noisy</i> data's variance that your k keeps, and the k the 50% rule would choose."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Leave σ = 4 and k = 12 (the starting values) and read the six readouts. Then press <i>Best k</i>, then <i>Variance target 50%</i>.","see":"Start: noisy input 15.98, error after PCA 7.27, error removed 54%, best k = 15 (error 7.15), variance kept 51.7%, and 12 for the 50% target. The two buttons put k at 15 and at 12, and the two errors (7.15 and 7.27) are within 2% of each other.","why":"The bottom of the U is flat: the signal thrown away by dropping a few components is about what the extra noise would add. Anywhere near the best k is almost as good."},{"do":"Set <i>Components kept, k</i> to 1 at σ = 4, then to 2.","see":"At k = 1 the error is 16.26, higher than the dashed line (15.98), and \"Error removed\" shows −2%. At k = 2 the error is 13.98.","why":"One component keeps only 14.9% of the digits' own variance. The signal thrown away (about 16 per pixel) costs more than the noise removed. The curve crosses below the dashed line at k = 2."},{"do":"Raise σ to 8, press <i>Best k</i>, then press <i>Variance target 50%</i>.","see":"Best k drops to 4 with error 14.15 (78% removed, from 63.91). The 50% target jumps to k = 22, where the error is 27.80, about twice the best.","why":"σ² = 64, and only λ₁ to λ₅ (179 to 69.5) exceed it, so only a handful of directions beat the noise. The variance target does not know σ, so it keeps 17 more components than the rule allows, and they are mostly noise."},{"do":"Set σ = 0.","see":"The blue curve falls steadily and is 0 from k = 61 on. There is no U. \"Best k\" reads 61, and \"Error removed\" shows a dash.","why":"With no noise there is nothing to trade: every extra component recovers signal. It stops at 61 because three pixel positions are 0 in every image, so only 61 directions carry any variance."},{"do":"Reload the page. At σ = 4 note \"Best k\", then press <i>New noise</i> twice, noting \"Best k\" after each press. Reload and repeat at σ = 6.","see":"At σ = 4 the best k is 15, 16, 15 over the first three noise samples. At σ = 6 it is 10, 8, 10.","why":"A new sample of noise nudges the estimated directions a little, so the best k moves by one or two. The whole curve, and the idea, stay the same."},{"do":"Move k to 64 at any σ.","see":"The blue curve meets the dashed line, \"Error after PCA at your k\" equals the noisy input's 15.98, and \"Error removed\" reads 0%. The bottom row of digits looks the same as the \"noisy\" row.","why":"Keeping all 64 directions is the identity: nothing is removed."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"<b>error ≈ (signal thrown away + noise kept) ÷ 64</b>, that is <span class=\"eq\">(Σⱼ>k λⱼ + k·σ²) / 64</span>. The first part falls with k, the second rises, and the U-shape is their sum."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Keep component j only while <b>λⱼ > σ²</b>. Counting clean eigenvalues above σ², for σ = 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 you get 47, 33, 16, 10, 5. The page's best k for its first noise sample is 48, 33, 15, 10, 4."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"Left arm of the U: blur, from too few components. Right arm: speckle, from too many. The bottom is flat. At σ = 4, k = 12 (error 7.27) is within 2% of k = 15 (7.15)."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"At k = 64 the error must equal the noisy input's, because nothing is removed. If your own denoising does not satisfy this, something else was changed (for example clipping the pixels)."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"You can estimate σ² from the tail of the noisy scree plot. At σ = 4, the last 20 eigenvalues of the noisy data average 14.8 against σ² = 16. It is a little low because the smallest eigenvalues of a sample are always pulled down."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>pca.inverse_transform(pca.transform(noisy))</code> compresses then expands: that is denoising. For a whole curve, fit once with all 64 components and slice: <code>Z[:, :k] @ pca.components_[:k] + pca.mean_</code>."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"More components always give a better picture.\"","right":"Better than the noisy picture only up to the best k. Against the clean digit the error is 7.15 at k = 15 but 15.98 at k = 64 (σ = 4): beyond the best k you let the noise back in."},{"wrong":"\"Keep 95% (or 50%) of the variance and the denoising is optimal.\"","right":"The target ignores σ. At σ = 4 the 50% rule gives 12, close to the best 15. At σ = 8 it gives 22 while the best is 4, with about twice the error."},{"wrong":"\"PCA needs clean data to find the right directions.\"","right":"Here it is fitted on the noisy images only. Independent noise adds σ² to every eigenvalue and leaves the eigenvectors alone, so the digit directions still come out on top."},{"wrong":"\"PCA removes all the noise.\"","right":"It removes the noise in the dropped directions only. In the k kept directions the noise stays. At σ = 4 and k = 15, that is 15 × 16 / 64 = 3.75 of the 7.15 error that remains."}],"code":[{"title":"Add noise, denoise with k components, measure the error","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data                              # clean images, grey levels 0 to 16\nnoisy = X + 4 * np.random.RandomState(0).normal(size=X.shape)   # noise with sigma = 4 on every pixel\nprint(\"noisy input:\", round(float(((noisy - X) ** 2).mean()), 1))\nfor k in (1, 5, 15, 30, 64):\n    pca = PCA(n_components=k).fit(noisy)            # learn directions from the NOISY images\n    den = pca.inverse_transform(pca.transform(noisy))   # keep k directions, rebuild 64 pixels\n    print(\"k =\", k, \"error vs clean:\", round(float(((den - X) ** 2).mean()), 1))","out":"noisy input: 15.9\nk = 1 error vs clean: 16.2\nk = 5 error vs clean: 9.9\nk = 15 error vs clean: 7.1\nk = 30 error vs clean: 8.9\nk = 64 error vs clean: 15.9","walk":[["np.random.RandomState(0).normal(size=X.shape)","A generator with a fixed seed (0) gives the same noise on every run. <code>.normal(size=X.shape)</code> draws one standard bell-curve number for every pixel of every image. Multiplying by 4 makes σ = 4."],["PCA(n_components=k).fit(noisy)","Learns the k leading directions from the <b>noisy</b> images only. PCA never sees the clean X."],["pca.transform(noisy)","Projects onto the k directions: k scores per image."],["pca.inverse_transform(...)","Maps the scores back to 64 pixels: mean image + scores × directions. This is the denoised image."],["((den - X) ** 2).mean()","The mean squared error against the <b>clean</b> X, averaged over all pixels of all images. This is the number on the page's vertical axis."],["k = 64","The error equals the noisy input's (15.9): keeping every direction removes nothing."]],"tip":"These numbers are close to the page's (16.3, 9.9, 7.1, 9.0, 16.0) but not identical, because the random noise is not the same noise."},{"title":"Find the best k with one PCA fit","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data\nnoisy = X + 4 * np.random.RandomState(0).normal(size=X.shape)\npca = PCA().fit(noisy)                            # one fit keeps all 64 directions\nZ = pca.transform(noisy)                          # scores on every direction\nerr = [((Z[:, :k] @ pca.components_[:k] + pca.mean_ - X) ** 2).mean() for k in range(1, 65)]\nprint(\"best k:\", int(np.argmin(err)) + 1, \"error there:\", round(float(min(err)), 2))\nprint(\"error at k = 12:\", round(float(err[11]), 2), \" at k = 64:\", round(float(err[63]), 2))","out":"best k: 15 error there: 7.09\nerror at k = 12: 7.21  at k = 64: 15.92","walk":[["PCA().fit(noisy)","One fit with all 64 directions, ranked by variance."],["Z = pca.transform(noisy)","The scores on all 64 directions: shape (1797, 64)."],["Z[:, :k] @ pca.components_[:k] + pca.mean_","The first k columns of scores times the first k directions, plus the mean image. It equals <code>inverse_transform</code> with k components, without refitting. <code>:k</code> means \"the first k\"."],["for k in range(1, 65)","Inside the square brackets this builds a list of 64 errors, one for each k from 1 to 64."],["np.argmin(err) + 1","The position of the smallest error. Add 1 because position 0 holds k = 1."]],"tip":"The page does exactly this: one PCA per noise level, then a slice for every k, which is why the whole curve appears at once."},{"title":"The rule \"keep λ > σ²\" and the noise floor","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = load_digits().data\nclean_var = PCA().fit(X).explained_variance_        # variance of each digit direction\nfor sigma in (1, 2, 4, 6, 8):\n    print(\"sigma\", sigma, \"-> keep\", int((clean_var > sigma**2).sum()))   # signal beats noise\nnoisy = X + 4 * np.random.RandomState(0).normal(size=X.shape)\ntail = PCA().fit(noisy).explained_variance_[-20:]   # the 20 smallest variances of the noisy data\nprint(\"tail average:\", round(float(tail.mean()), 1), \"vs sigma^2 = 16\")","out":"sigma 1 -> keep 47\nsigma 2 -> keep 33\nsigma 4 -> keep 16\nsigma 6 -> keep 10\nsigma 8 -> keep 5\ntail average: 14.8 vs sigma^2 = 16","walk":[["PCA().fit(X).explained_variance_","The 64 variances λⱼ of the <b>clean</b> digits, largest first."],["clean_var > sigma**2","True for every direction whose signal variance beats the noise variance σ². <code>**</code> is the power."],[".sum()","Adding up Trues counts them: the number of directions worth keeping."],["explained_variance_[-20:]","The last 20 entries, the weakest directions of the noisy data."],["tail.mean()","About 14.8, a little under σ² = 16. The tail is dominated by noise, so it estimates σ²."]],"tip":"The rule needs the clean variances, so in practice you use it with an estimate of σ² (for example from the tail) or just cross-validate k."}],"check":[{"q":"Why can keeping only the top PCA components remove noise from digit images?","options":["PCA applies a blur filter to the image","PCA finds the random pixels and sets them to zero","The noise is spread equally over all directions while the digit lives in a few, so the weak directions hold mostly noise","Noise always has smaller pixel values than the digit"],"answer":2,"why":"Independent noise adds the same variance σ² to every direction. A digit has large variance in a few directions and almost none in the rest, so in the weak directions there is mostly noise, and dropping them removes it."},{"q":"The noise level is σ = 3, so σ² = 9. The signal variances of the first five directions are [40, 20, 12, 8, 3]. How many components should you keep?","options":["3","5","4","2"],"answer":0,"why":"Keep a component while its signal variance beats the noise variance: 40, 20 and 12 are above 9, but 8 is below. So keep 3."},{"q":"On the page you raise σ from 4 to 8. What happens to \"Best k\"?","options":["It falls, from 15 to 4","It stays at 15","It rises, because there is more noise to remove","It becomes 64"],"answer":0,"why":"More noise raises σ² from 16 to 64, so fewer directions have signal variance above it. The best k falls to 4. Removing noise by keeping more components would do the opposite: it keeps more noise."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why keeping only the top k PCA components removes noise, and why the best k gets smaller when the noise grows.","model":"A digit's variation is concentrated in a few directions, while random noise adds the same variance σ² to every direction, so the weak directions hold only noise and dropping them removes it. Each kept direction brings its signal variance λ but also σ² of noise, so it pays off only while λ > σ², which means more noise leaves fewer directions worth keeping."},"cheat":"Noise adds σ² to every direction, while digits keep their variance λⱼ in a few. Keeping k components leaves an error per pixel of about (Σⱼ>k λⱼ + k·σ²)/64: signal thrown away plus noise kept, a U-shape. The minimum is near λⱼ ≈ σ², so keep components with λⱼ > σ². More noise, smaller best k. A fixed variance target ignores σ."},"pca/pipeline-cv":{"idea":"To choose how many principal components to keep, score each k by cross-validation, with scaling and PCA fitted inside every fold on the training rows only. Read the accuracy curve with its spread, then take the simplest k that is still near the top.","why":"If PCA or scaling sees the held-out rows before the exam, the exam is no longer fair, and you cannot trust the score you used to choose k. If you pick the top bar of a noisy curve, you pay for complexity that buys only luck. The pipeline and the spread guard against both.","analogy":"A student prepares from a textbook, then sits an exam. Cheating is not only copying the answers: it is also letting the exam papers shape what you studied. Scaling and PCA are part of the studying. Each fold is a new exam, and the studying (scaler, PCA, model) is redone from scratch with that exam's papers locked away.","vocab":[["Hyper-parameter","A setting you choose before training, such as k. The model does not learn it from the data, you tune it."],["Cross-validation (5-fold)","Split the rows into 5 folds. Train on 4, score on the 5th, five times, so each fold is the exam once. You get 5 scores."],["Stratified","Each fold keeps about the same share of each cultivar as the whole data. Wine: 178 rows, folds of 36, 36, 36, 35, 35."],["Pipeline","Scale, then PCA, then logistic regression, run as one object. Calling <code>fit</code> fits every step on the rows you give it, in order."],["Leakage","Information from the held-out rows reaching the fitting of anything, here the scaler or PCA. It makes the score look better, or just different, than it will be on new data."],["Spread (std)","The standard deviation of the 5 fold accuracies. It tells you how much the score moves with which wines are held out."],["Accuracy","The share of wines whose cultivar is predicted correctly. One wine out of 178 is 0.56 percentage points."]],"steps":[{"h":"The decision","p":"Wine has 13 chemical measurements for 178 wines of 3 cultivars. A model can use all 13 columns, or only the first k principal components of the standardised data. Fewer inputs can make a model simpler and steadier. But how many is enough? k is a <b>hyper-parameter</b>: you choose it, the model does not learn it."},{"h":"Why not judge k on the training data","p":"Fit and score on the same 178 wines and accuracy climbs to 100% at k = 7 and stays there. It cannot tell k = 7 from k = 13, and it says nothing about new wines, because more inputs only give the model more to memorise. You need a score from wines the model did not train on, for every k you want to compare."},{"h":"Cross-validation gives that score","p":"Split the 178 wines into 5 stratified folds. Train on 4 folds, score on the fifth, and repeat so each fold is the exam once. You get 5 accuracies. Their mean is the score for this k. Their spread (standard deviation) shows how much it moves with which wines are held out."},{"h":"Put every step in one pipeline","p":"The pipeline is scale → PCA(k) → logistic regression. In each round all three steps are <b>fitted on the 4 training folds only</b>. The held-out fold is merely <i>transformed</i> with the scaling and PCA learned from the training folds, then predicted. This mimics real use: new wines arrive after the preparation was fixed."},{"h":"What leakage looks like","p":"Suppose you scale (or run PCA) on all 178 wines first and cross-validate afterwards. The held-out wines have then shaped the mean, sd and directions the model uses, so the exam papers influenced the studying. On wine the effect is tiny and its sign is random. That does not make the procedure acceptable: the correct way costs nothing extra, so use it every time."},{"h":"Read the curve with its spread","p":"Plot the mean accuracy against k, with ±1 standard deviation as bars. Look for where the curve stops climbing. Here it jumps from 84.9% at k = 1 to 95.5% at k = 2, then creeps up. One wine is 0.56 points overall and 2.8 points inside a fold of 36, so differences smaller than the bars are noise."},{"h":"Choose k with two rules","p":"<b>Best mean:</b> k = 11 at 98.9%. <b>Simplest within one spread:</b> the smallest k whose mean is at least (best mean − best spread) = 98.9 − 1.4 = 97.5. That is k = 6 (97.8%). A stricter version divides the spread by √5 to get 0.6, so the threshold is 98.3, and it picks k = 9. The page shows both rules. When two settings cannot be told apart, prefer the simpler one."},{"h":"After choosing","p":"The cross-validated score helped choose k, so it is a little optimistic for the final model. Then refit the whole pipeline with the chosen k on all the training data, and report one last score on a test set that played no part in the choice. The page shows only the selection step. It has no test set."}],"example":{"title":"one row of the sweep, by hand","setup":"The page cross-validates every k with 5 folds of 36, 36, 36, 35 and 35 wines. Here is how many wines each fold got right at k = 6 and at k = 11. Compute the mean and spread for k = 11, then choose between the two.","table":{"head":["Fold","Wines in fold","Correct at k = 11","Accuracy","Correct at k = 6","Accuracy"],"rows":[["1","36","35","97.22%","35","97.22%"],["2","36","35","97.22%","34","94.44%"],["3","36","36","100%","35","97.22%"],["4","35","35","100%","35","100%"],["5","35","35","100%","35","100%"]]},"walk":["<b>Accuracy per fold</b> = correct / wines. At k = 11: 35/36 = 97.22%, 97.22%, 36/36 = 100%, 100%, 100%.","<b>Mean</b> = (97.22 + 97.22 + 100 + 100 + 100) / 5 = 494.44 / 5 = <b>98.89%</b>.","<b>Spread.</b> Deviations from the mean: −1.67, −1.67, +1.11, +1.11, +1.11. Squares: 2.78, 2.78, 1.23, 1.23, 1.23, which add to 9.26. Divide by 5: 1.852. Square root: <b>1.36</b>. (scikit-learn's <code>.std()</code> and the page divide by 5, not 4.)","<b>Same for k = 6.</b> Accuracies 97.22, 94.44, 97.22, 100, 100 give mean <b>97.78%</b> and spread <b>2.08</b>.","<b>Apply the rule.</b> Threshold = best mean − best spread = 98.89 − 1.36 = 97.53. Smaller k: k = 5 has 97.21, below the threshold. k = 6 has 97.78, above it. So the simplest k within one spread is 6.","<b>Count wines.</b> k = 11 gets 2 wines wrong out of 178, and k = 6 gets 4 wrong. The gap is 2 wines, 1.1 points, smaller than either spread."],"result":"Best mean: k = 11 (98.9 ± 1.4%). Simplest within one spread: k = 6 (97.8 ± 2.1%). Choosing k = 6 gives up two wines out of 178, a gap that fold-to-fold noise can easily produce, and uses 6 inputs instead of 11."},"read":[["Solid blue line with circles: Honest pipeline","Mean accuracy over the 5 folds for each k from 1 to 13. The vertical bars are ±1 standard deviation across the folds."],["Dashed orange line with triangles: Leaky scaler","The scaler is fitted on all 178 rows before cross-validation. Drawn only after you tick the checkbox."],["Dotted green line with squares: Leaky scaler + PCA","Both the scaler and PCA are fitted on all 178 rows. Drawn only after you tick the checkbox."],["Ring and label \"best k = N\"","The k with the highest mean accuracy on the honest line (ties go to the smaller k)."],["Hovering over a k","A tooltip with the honest mean ± spread, the accuracy of each of the 5 folds, and the leaky means if they have been run."],["Table view","The same numbers as a table. Open it under the plot."],["Checkbox: Also run the two leaky versions","Adds the two leaky curves, their legend entries and table columns. It takes longer, shown by the progress bar. Unticking hides them at once (the results stay in memory)."],["Button: Run the sweep","Clears the results and recomputes everything. The numbers come out identical, because the folds are fixed."],["Readouts: Best k (highest mean accuracy), Smallest k within one spread (fold sd) of best, Smallest k within one standard error of best","The three choice rules from the steps. The second takes the smallest k whose mean is at least best mean − best spread. The third allows only spread ÷ √5, so it may pick a larger k."],["Readouts: All 13 components (no reduction), Just k = 1","Two reference points: the same model with no PCA reduction, and with a single component."],["Readout: Leaky minus honest, at best k","The accuracy of the leaky-scaler curve minus the honest one at the best k, in percentage points. Until you tick the leaky box it asks you to tick it."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Wait for the progress bar to finish (the page runs the honest sweep on load). Read the five readouts, then hover over k = 1 and over k = 11.","see":"Best k = 11 (98.9%). Smallest k within one spread = 6 (97.8%). Within one standard error = 9 (98.3%). All 13 components: 98.3%. Just k = 1: 84.9%. The tooltip at k = 1 reads 84.9% ± 6.0 with folds 83, 89, 78, 80, 94%. At k = 11 it reads 98.9% ± 1.4 with folds 97, 97, 100, 100, 100%.","why":"Each fold is 35 or 36 wines, so one wine moves a fold by about 2.8 points. At k = 1 the folds differ by 16 points. At k = 11 only two wines are wrong in total."},{"do":"Follow the blue curve from k = 1 to k = 13 and note where the steep climb ends.","see":"The mean is 84.9, 95.5, 96.1, 94.4, 97.2% for k = 1 to 5, then 97.8% for k = 6 to 8, 98.3% for 9 and 10, 98.9% for 11 and 12, and 98.3% at 13. The steep part ends at k = 2. The dip at k = 4 is smaller than its bar.","why":"The first two components carry the main chemical differences between the cultivars. Later ones add small refinements and some noise. Wiggles smaller than the bars (k = 4 is 1.7 below k = 3, with bars of 3.5 to 4.2) are chance."},{"do":"Tick <i>Also run the two leaky versions</i> and wait. Then hover over k = 2, k = 5 and k = 11.","see":"Two more curves appear. \"Leaky minus honest, at best k\" reads +0.00 points. Over all k the leaky scaler differs from honest by −0.56 to +1.11 points: +1.11 at k = 2 and 3 (96.6% against 95.5% at k = 2), −0.56 at k = 5, 6 and 7, and about +0.09 on average.","why":"The scaler's means and sds from 178 rows hardly differ from those of the 142 or 143 training rows, so the leak is tiny, and which way it tips a borderline wine is luck. Not seeing the leak in the numbers does not make the procedure acceptable. On other data the same shortcut can give a large, hidden optimism."},{"do":"Look at the readout \"Just k = 1\" and compare it with guessing.","see":"84.9% from a single number per wine, against 33.3% for a random guess among three cultivars and 39.9% for always answering the biggest cultivar (71 of 178).","why":"PC1 of the standardised wines separates the cultivars: the mean PC1 score is +2.3 for cultivar 1, 0.0 for cultivar 2 and −2.7 for cultivar 3, with spreads of 0.6 to 1.3 inside each. One axis already sorts most wines."},{"do":"Press <i>Run the sweep</i> again.","see":"The curve and every readout come out exactly the same as before.","why":"The folds are fixed (stratified, no shuffling, the same as scikit-learn's default) and logistic regression has no randomness. The spread bars measure how the score changes between folds, not between runs."},{"do":"Compare k = 6, k = 11 and k = 13 using the tooltips and the table view.","see":"The means are 97.8%, 98.9% and 98.3%, which is 4, 2 and 3 wines wrong out of 178. The spreads are 2.1, 1.4 and 1.4.","why":"The whole range from k = 6 to k = 13 is 2 wines. That is why the page offers \"smallest k within one spread\": a bigger model that wins by 2 wines has not proven it is better."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Five exams. Each fold is an exam paper locked away while the pipeline (scaler, PCA, model) studies from the other four. The exam papers never touch the studying."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>Fit on train, transform on test.</b> Anything with a <code>fit</code> belongs inside the pipeline. The held-out fold only ever goes through <code>transform</code> and <code>predict</code>."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"To audit for leakage, ask of every step: \"Was its <code>fit</code> ever called on rows that include the held-out fold?\" One yes is a leak, even for steps that never look at labels."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Differences smaller than the spread are noise. One wine is 0.56 points of 178, so a spread of ±1.4 is about two and a half wines."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Mean ± spread over the folds for each k. Take the smallest k with mean ≥ (best mean − best spread)."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Always compare with the dumbest baseline. Always answering the biggest cultivar gets 39.9% here. A score near that means the model learned nothing."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>GridSearchCV(pipe, {\"pca__n_components\": range(1, 14)}, cv=5)</code>. The double underscore means \"the step named pca, its parameter n_components\"."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The k with the highest accuracy is the right k.\"","right":"Highest by 1 or 2 wines out of 178 is within noise. Take the simplest k whose mean is within one spread of the best, here k = 6 rather than k = 11."},{"wrong":"\"Leaky and honest curves look the same here, so leakage does not matter.\"","right":"On wine the leak is tiny and random in sign (−0.56 to +1.11 points). It is a procedure error, wrong even when harmless, and you cannot know in advance when it will be large."},{"wrong":"\"PCA and scaling do not use labels, so they cannot leak.\"","right":"They use the features of the held-out rows, so those rows shape the mean, sd and directions. The leak is smaller than one that uses labels, but it is still a leak, and the pipeline removes it for free."},{"wrong":"\"The error bars are the uncertainty of the true accuracy.\"","right":"They are the spread of 5 fold scores, and the folds share most of their training rows. Use them as a rough yardstick for \"is this difference bigger than noise\", not as an exact interval."}],"code":[{"title":"Cross-validate the whole pipeline for a few values of k","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nX, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)\nfor k in (1, 2, 6, 11, 13):\n    pipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=k), LogisticRegression(max_iter=10000, tol=1e-8))\n    s = cross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=5)       # five times: fit on 4 folds, score on the 5th\n    print(k, round(s.mean(), 4), round(s.std(), 4))","out":"1 0.8486 0.0602\n2 0.9551 0.0223\n6 0.9778 0.0208\n11 0.9889 0.0136\n13 0.9832 0.0137","walk":[["make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(n_components=k), LogisticRegression(...))","Chains three steps. <code>fit</code> on the pipeline fits the scaler, then PCA on the scaled output, then the classifier on the PCA scores."],["LogisticRegression(max_iter=10000, tol=1e-8)","The classifier. <code>max_iter</code> caps the optimiser's steps. <code>tol=1e-8</code> is a strict stopping rule so the numbers match the page. The default (1e-4) stops earlier, and at k = 5 it flips one borderline wine."],["cross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=5)","For each of 5 folds: clone the pipeline, fit it on the other 4 folds, score accuracy on this one. Returns 5 numbers. For a classifier, <code>cv=5</code> means stratified folds without shuffling."],["s.mean(), s.std()","The mean accuracy and the spread. <code>s.std()</code> divides by 5, as the page does."],["X, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)","Features first, labels second. Note that X is <b>raw</b>: the scaling happens inside the pipeline, per fold."]],"tip":"The five lines for k = 1, 2, 6, 11 and 13 reproduce the page's honest curve exactly."},{"title":"Let GridSearchCV try every k, then apply the one-spread rule","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nX, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)\npipe = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=10000, tol=1e-8))\ng = GridSearchCV(pipe, {\"pca__n_components\": range(1, 14)}, cv=5).fit(X, y)\nm, s, b = g.cv_results_[\"mean_test_score\"], g.cv_results_[\"std_test_score\"], g.best_index_\nprint(\"best k:\", b + 1, \"mean:\", round(m[b], 4), \"spread:\", round(s[b], 4))\nprint(\"smallest k within one spread:\", next(i + 1 for i in range(13) if m[i] >= m[b] - s[b]))","out":"best k: 11 mean: 0.9889 spread: 0.0136\nsmallest k within one spread: 6","walk":[["PCA()","No <code>n_components</code> here. GridSearchCV will set it for each trial."],["{\"pca__n_components\": range(1, 14)}","The grid. A parameter inside a pipeline is named <i>step name</i>, two underscores, <i>parameter</i>. The step is called \"pca\" because <code>make_pipeline</code> names it after its class. It tries k = 1, …, 13."],["GridSearchCV(pipe, grid, cv=5).fit(X, y)","For every k, runs the 5-fold cross-validation with the whole pipeline refitted inside each fold, so nothing leaks. Afterwards it also refits the best pipeline on all rows."],["cv_results_[\"mean_test_score\"], [\"std_test_score\"]","The mean and the spread of the 5 fold scores for each k, in grid order."],["g.best_index_","The position of the best mean. Ties go to the first, so k = 11 rather than 12."],["next(i + 1 for i in range(13) if m[i] >= m[b] - s[b])","The first position whose mean is at least best mean − best spread. Add 1 to turn a position into k. This is the page's \"smallest k within one spread\"."]],"tip":"The refitted best pipeline is in <code>g.best_estimator_</code>, ready to score on a test set you kept aside."},{"title":"The leak: scale first, cross-validate after","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nX, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)\nlr = LogisticRegression(max_iter=10000, tol=1e-8)\nleaky = cross_val_score(make_pipeline(PCA(2), lr), StandardScaler().fit_transform(X), y, cv=5).mean()   # scaler saw every row\nhonest = cross_val_score(make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(2), lr), X, y, cv=5).mean()\nprint(\"leaky:\", round(leaky, 4), \"honest:\", round(honest, 4))","out":"leaky: 0.9662 honest: 0.9551","walk":[["StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)","Leaky: the scaler learns its means and sds from <b>all</b> 178 wines, including those that will later be held out."],["make_pipeline(PCA(2), lr) on the scaled data","Cross-validation then splits already-scaled data, so the held-out rows have shaped the scaling."],["make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), PCA(2), lr) on X","Honest: the scaler is a pipeline step, so it is fitted inside each fold on the training rows only."],["lr","One classifier object shared by both pipelines. Cross-validation clones it for every fold, so nothing carries over."],[".mean()","The average accuracy of the 5 folds."]],"tip":"Here the leaky number is higher (+1.1 points at k = 2), yet at k = 5 it is lower (96.7% against 97.2%). The sign is luck. Never accept a leaky protocol because the number looks fine."}],"check":[{"q":"You cross-validate a Pipeline(StandardScaler, PCA, LogisticRegression). When is the scaler fitted?","options":["Separately in each fold, on that fold's training rows only","On the held-out fold, so that it matches the exam data","Once, on all rows, before the folds are made","Never: a scaler only transforms"],"answer":0,"why":"Each cross-validation round fits the whole pipeline, scaler included, on the training folds. The held-out fold is only transformed with what the training folds taught the scaler."},{"q":"At k = 11 the fold accuracies are 97.2, 97.2, 100, 100, 100 (mean 98.9, spread 1.4). Another k has mean 97.8. Is it within one spread of the best?","options":["Yes: 97.8 ≥ 98.9 − 1.4 = 97.5","It cannot be told without a test set","No: the spread of the other k is bigger","No: 97.8 is below 98.9"],"answer":0,"why":"The rule is: mean ≥ best mean − best spread. 98.9 − 1.4 = 97.5, and 97.8 is above that. The two means differ by only 2 wines out of 178."},{"q":"You tick \"Also run the two leaky versions\" and \"Leaky minus honest, at best k\" shows +0.00 points. What should you conclude?","options":["On this small, clean dataset the leak was too small to show, but the pipeline is still the correct procedure","The leaky version is the correct one","The pipeline is broken","Leakage never matters, so you can scale first"],"answer":0,"why":"The leak is real in principle but tiny here, and its sign is random (other k values show −0.56 to +1.11 points). A harmless result does not make the shortcut valid, and the correct procedure costs nothing extra."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why the scaler and PCA go inside the cross-validated pipeline, and how you pick k from the curve.","model":"Anything that learns from the data, like the scaler's means or PCA's directions, must be learned from the training folds only and merely applied to the held-out fold, otherwise the exam wines have already shaped the preparation. For each k I compute the mean and spread of the fold accuracies and take the smallest k whose mean is within one spread of the best, because differences smaller than the spread are noise."},"cheat":"Pipeline = scale → PCA(k) → classifier, every step fitted on the training folds only; held-out folds are only transformed. 5-fold CV gives a mean and a spread per k. Wine: k = 1 gives 84.9%, best k = 11 (98.9 ± 1.4), simplest within one spread k = 6 (97.8). Scaling or PCA on all rows first is a leak, even when harmless, as here."}});
